 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ for the decision logic; $O(N)$ for the actual voxelization, where $N$ is the number of primitives or points. The effective time per frame is reduced by the update frequency factor $f < 1$.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$, where $V$ is the number of voxels, same as synchronous methods. No additional memory overhead for the timing logic.
-- **Measured Performance:** In voxel cone tracing implementations, updating every 5 frames significantly reduces GPU load, allowing for real-time global illumination with dynamic lights [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Reduces computational load; leverages temporal coherence; suitable for static or slow-moving scenes.
-- **Limitations:** Introduces visual artifacts (jitter, lag) with fast-moving geometry or lighting; not suitable for high-fidelity static scenes where temporal coherence is low.
-- **When to Use:** Real-time rendering with dynamic lighting where performance is critical and minor visual artifacts are acceptable.
-- **When Not to Use:** High-precision offline rendering, scenes with fast-moving objects, or when visual fidelity is paramount.
+- **Time Complexity:** Reduces the number of voxelization passes by a factor of $N$ (where $N$ is the ratio of render frame rate to update frequency), effectively lowering the computational load per second by $1 - 1/N$.
+- **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory overhead compared to synchronous voxelization, as the same voxel grid structure is reused.
+- **Measured Performance:** In implementations using voxel cone tracing, updating every 5 frames significantly reduces processing power usage, enabling real-time global illumination with dynamic lighting [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** High performance improvement for static or slow-moving scenes; leverages temporal coherency.
+- **Limitations:** Introduces latency between scene changes and their representation in the voxel grid, causing visual artifacts if objects move quickly [S2, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:** Ideal for scenes with static geometry and lighting, or where performance is critical and minor visual lag is acceptable.
+- **When Not to Use:** Unsuitable for fast-moving dynamic scenes or when high-fidelity real-time geometric accuracy is required.
 
 ## Background
-Asynchronous Voxel Update was introduced in the context of implementing real-time Voxel Cone Tracing for global illumination [S2, unofficial]. The technique was proposed to mitigate the high computational cost of voxelizing dynamic scenes every frame, allowing for single-bounce indirect lighting with directional lights in real-time applications [S2, unofficial]. It is commonly used in game engines and real-time rendering demos where performance optimization is critical [S1, unofficial].
+The technique was described in the context of implementing voxel cone tracing for real-time global illumination, where it was noted that voxel worlds can be updated at a different frequency than the render frame rate to exploit temporal coherency of light sources and dynamic models [S2, unofficial]. It is also referenced in the Brush Gaussian splatting engine, where update logic is gated by progress and time intervals to manage caching and rendering resources [S1, unofficial].
 
